window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2021-06-3", "source": "2021年06月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions:For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay titled “Doviolent video games lead to violence?”. The statement given below is for your reference. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words .\n\nA growing body of research finds that violent video games can make kids act aggressively in their real world relationships, causing an increase in violence.", "sample": "Do violent video games lead to violence? While a growing body of research suggests such games can make\nplayers more aggressive, I believe they do not directly cause real violence; rather, their influence depends on\nthe individual and the surrounding environment.\nTo begin with, exposure to violent scenes may indeed shape behavior. First, children who spend hours shooting\nand fighting on screen can gradually treat aggression as normal, and some may copy what they see when they\nlose their temper. However, most players clearly separate games from reality. A well-supervised teenager, for\ninstance, can enjoy an action game after homework and still behave kindly toward classmates, because family\nguidance and self-control matter far more than the game itself.\nIn conclusion, violent video games alone do not turn ordinary people into violent ones. What truly counts is\nproper parental supervision and healthy habits. Therefore, rather than simply blaming the games, parents and\nschools should teach children to play in moderation and to tell fantasy from real life."}, "translation": {"prompt": "龙井(Longjing)是一种绿茶，主要产自中国东部沿海的浙江省。龙井茶独特的香味和口感为其赢得了\"中国名茶\"的称号，在中国深受大众的欢迎，在海外饮用的人也越来越多。龙井茶通常手工制作，其价格可能极其昂贵，也可能比较便宜，这取决于茶的生长地、采摘时间和制作工艺。龙井茶富含维生素C和其他多种有益健康的元素。经常喝龙井茶有助于减轻疲劳、延缓衰老。", "sample": "Longjing is a kind of green tea produced mainly in Zhejiang Province on the eastern coast of China. Its unique aroma and taste have earned Longjing the title of \"China Famous Tea\", and it is very popular among the public in China, with an increasing number of people drinking it abroad as well. Longjing tea is usually made by hand, and its price can be extremely high or relatively low, depending on where it grows, when it is picked, and how it is made. In addition, Longjing tea is rich in vitamin C and various other elements beneficial to health. Drinking it regularly helps relieve fatigue and delay aging."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "Nowadays you can't buy anything without being asked to provide a rating of a company's performance on a five-star scale.\nI've been asked to rate my \"store  __26__  \" on the EFTPOS terminal before I can pay. Even the most  __27__  activities, such as calling Telstra or picking up a parcel from Australia Post, are followed by texts or emails with surveys asking, \"How did we do?\"\nOnline purchases are  __28__  followed up by a customer satisfaction survey. Companies are so  __29__  for a hit of stars that if you delete the survey the company sends you another one.\nWe're  __30__  to rate our apps when we've barely had a chance to use them. One online course provider I use asks you what you think of the course after you've only completed  __31__  2 per cent of it.\nEconomist Jason Murphy says that companies use customer satisfaction ratings because a  __32__  display of star feedback has become the nuclear power source of the modern economy.\nHowever, you can't help but  __33__  if these companies are basing their business on fabrications(捏造的东西). I  __34__  that with online surveys I just click the  __35__  that's closest to my mouse cursor(光标) to get the damn thing off my screen. Often the star rating I give has far more to do with the kind of day I'm having than the purchase I just made.", "matchIntro": "Science of setbacks: How failure can improve career prospects", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "How do early career setbacks affect our long-term success? Failures can help us learn and overcome our fears. But disasters can still wound us. They can screw us up and set us back. Wouldn't it be nice if there was genuine, scientifically documented truth to the expression \"what doesn't kill you makes you stronger\"?"}, {"label": "B", "text": "One way social scientists have probed the effects of career setbacks is to look at scientists of very similar qualifications. These scientists, for reasons that are mostly arbitrary, either just missed getting a research grant or just barely made it. In social sciences, this is known as examining \"near misses\" and \"narrow wins\" in areas where merit is subjective. That allows researchers to measure only the effects of being chosen or not. Studies in this area have found conflicting results. In the competitive game of biomedical science, research has been done on scientists who narrowly lost or won grant money. It suggests that narrow winners become even bigger winners down the line. In other words, the rich get richer."}, {"label": "C", "text": "A 2018 study published in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, for example, followed researchers in the Netherlands. Researchers concluded that those who just barely qualified for a grant were able to get twice as much money within the next eight years as those who just missed out. And the narrow winners were 50 percent more likely to be given a professorship."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Others in the US have found similar effects with National Institutes of Health early-career fellowships launching narrow winners far ahead of close losers. The phenomenon is often referred to as the Matthew effect, inspired by the Bible's wisdom that to those who have, more will be given. There's a good explanation for the phenomenon in the book The Formula: The Universal Laws of Success by Albert Laszlo Barabasi. According to Barabasi, it's easier and less risky for those in positions of power to choose to hand awards and funding to those who've already been so recognized."}, {"label": "E", "text": "This is bad news for the losers. Small early career setbacks seem to have a disproportionate effect down the line. What didn't kill them made them weaker. But other studies using the same technique have shown there's sometimes no penalty to a near miss. Students who just miss getting into top high schools or universities do just as well later in life as those who just manage to get accepted. In this case, what didn't kill them simply didn't matter. So is there any evidence that setbacks might actually improve our career prospects? There is now."}, {"label": "F", "text": "In a study published in Nature Communications, Northwestern University sociologist Dashun Wang tracked more than 1,100 scientists who were on the border between getting a grant and missing out between 1990 and 2005. He followed various measures of performance over the next decade. These included how many papers they authored and how influential those papers were, as measured by the number of subsequent citations. As expected, there was a much higher rate of attrition(减员) among scientists who didn't get grants. But among those who stayed on, the close losers performed even better than the narrow winners. To make sure this wasn't by chance, Wang conducted additional tests using different performance measures. He examined how many times people were first authors on influential studies, and the like."}, {"label": "G", "text": "One straightforward reason close losers might outperform narrow winners is that the two groups have comparable ability. In Wang's study, he selected the most determined, passionate scientists from the loser group and culled(剔除) what he deemed the weakest members of the winner group. Yet the persevering losers still came out on top. He thinks that being a close loser might give people a psychological boost, or the proverbial kick in the pants."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Utrecht University sociologist Arnout van de Rijt was the lead author on the 2018 paper showing the rich get richer. He said the new finding is apparently reasonable and worth some attention. His own work showed that although the narrow winners did get much more money in the near future, the actual performance of the close losers was just as good."}, {"label": "I", "text": "He said the people who should be paying regard to the Wang paper are the funding agents who distribute government grant money. After all, by continuing to pile riches on the narrow winners, the taxpayers are not getting the maximum bang for their buck if the close losers are performing just as well or even better. There's a huge amount of time and effort that goes into the process of selecting who gets grants, he said, and the latest research shows that the scientific establishment is not very good at distributing money. \"Maybe we should spend less money trying to figure out who is better than who,\" he said, suggesting that some more equal dividing up of money might be more productive and more efficient. Van de Rijt said he's not convinced that losing out gives people a psychological boost. It may yet be a selection effect. Even though Wang tried to account for this by culling the weakest winners, it's impossible to know which of the winners would have quit had they found themselves on the losing side."}, {"label": "J", "text": "For his part, Wang said that in his own experience, losing did light a motivating fire. He recalled a recent paper he submitted to a journal, which accepted it only to request extensive editing, and then reversed course and rejected it. He submitted the unedited version to a more respected journal and got accepted."}, {"label": "K", "text": "In sports and many areas of life, we think of failures as evidence of something we could have done better. We regard these disappointments as a fate we could have avoided with more careful preparation, different training, a better strategy, or more focus. And there it makes sense that failures show us the road to success. These papers deal with a kind of failure people have little control over—rejection. Others determine who wins and who loses. But at the very least, the research is starting to show that early setbacks don't have to be fatal. They might even make us better at our jobs. Getting paid like a winner, though? That's a different matter."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Boredom has become trendy. Studies point to how boredom is good for creativity and innovation, as well as mental health. It is found that people are more creative following the completion of a tedious task. When people are bored, they have an increase in \"associative thought\"—the process of making new connections between ideas, which is linked to innovative thinking. These studies are impressive, but in reality, the benefits of boredom may be related to having time to clear your mind, be quiet, or daydream.\nIn our stimulation-rich world, it seems unrealistic that boredom could occur at all. Yet, there are valid reasons boredom may feel so painful. As it turns out, boredom might signal the fact that you have a need that isn't being met.\nOur always-on world of social media may result in more connections, but they are superficial and can get in the way of building a real sense of belonging. Feeling bored may signal the desire for a greater sense of community and the feeling that you fit in with others around you. So take the step of joining an organization to build face-to-face relationships. You'll find depth that you won't get from your screen no matter how many likes you get on your post.\nSimilar to the need for belonging, bored people often report that they feel a limited sense of meaning. It's a fundamental human need to have a larger purpose and to feel like we're part of something bigger than ourselves. When people are bored, they're more likely to feel less meaning in their lives. If you want to reduce boredom and increase your sense of meaning, seek work where you can make a unique contribution, or find a cause you can support with your time and talent.\nIf your definition of boredom is being quiet, mindful, and reflective, keep it up. But if you're struggling with real boredom and the emptiness it provokes, consider whether you might seek new connections and more significant challenges. These are the things that will genuinely relieve boredom and make you more effective in the process.", "47": "Boredom has become trendy. Studies point to how boredom is good for creativity and innovation, as well as mental health. It is found that people are more creative following the completion of a tedious task. When people are bored, they have an increase in \"associative thought\"—the process of making new connections between ideas, which is linked to innovative thinking. These studies are impressive, but in reality, the benefits of boredom may be related to having time to clear your mind, be quiet, or daydream.\nIn our stimulation-rich world, it seems unrealistic that boredom could occur at all. Yet, there are valid reasons boredom may feel so painful. As it turns out, boredom might signal the fact that you have a need that isn't being met.\nOur always-on world of social media may result in more connections, but they are superficial and can get in the way of building a real sense of belonging. Feeling bored may signal the desire for a greater sense of community and the feeling that you fit in with others around you. So take the step of joining an organization to build face-to-face relationships. You'll find depth that you won't get from your screen no matter how many likes you get on your post.\nSimilar to the need for belonging, bored people often report that they feel a limited sense of meaning. It's a fundamental human need to have a larger purpose and to feel like we're part of something bigger than ourselves. When people are bored, they're more likely to feel less meaning in their lives. If you want to reduce boredom and increase your sense of meaning, seek work where you can make a unique contribution, or find a cause you can support with your time and talent.\nIf your definition of boredom is being quiet, mindful, and reflective, keep it up. But if you're struggling with real boredom and the emptiness it provokes, consider whether you might seek new connections and more significant challenges. These are the things that will genuinely relieve boredom and make you more effective in the process.", "48": "Boredom has become trendy. Studies point to how boredom is good for creativity and innovation, as well as mental health. It is found that people are more creative following the completion of a tedious task. When people are bored, they have an increase in \"associative thought\"—the process of making new connections between ideas, which is linked to innovative thinking. These studies are impressive, but in reality, the benefits of boredom may be related to having time to clear your mind, be quiet, or daydream.\nIn our stimulation-rich world, it seems unrealistic that boredom could occur at all. Yet, there are valid reasons boredom may feel so painful. As it turns out, boredom might signal the fact that you have a need that isn't being met.\nOur always-on world of social media may result in more connections, but they are superficial and can get in the way of building a real sense of belonging. Feeling bored may signal the desire for a greater sense of community and the feeling that you fit in with others around you. So take the step of joining an organization to build face-to-face relationships. You'll find depth that you won't get from your screen no matter how many likes you get on your post.\nSimilar to the need for belonging, bored people often report that they feel a limited sense of meaning. It's a fundamental human need to have a larger purpose and to feel like we're part of something bigger than ourselves. When people are bored, they're more likely to feel less meaning in their lives. If you want to reduce boredom and increase your sense of meaning, seek work where you can make a unique contribution, or find a cause you can support with your time and talent.\nIf your definition of boredom is being quiet, mindful, and reflective, keep it up. But if you're struggling with real boredom and the emptiness it provokes, consider whether you might seek new connections and more significant challenges. These are the things that will genuinely relieve boredom and make you more effective in the process.", "49": "Boredom has become trendy. Studies point to how boredom is good for creativity and innovation, as well as mental health. It is found that people are more creative following the completion of a tedious task. When people are bored, they have an increase in \"associative thought\"—the process of making new connections between ideas, which is linked to innovative thinking. These studies are impressive, but in reality, the benefits of boredom may be related to having time to clear your mind, be quiet, or daydream.\nIn our stimulation-rich world, it seems unrealistic that boredom could occur at all. Yet, there are valid reasons boredom may feel so painful. As it turns out, boredom might signal the fact that you have a need that isn't being met.\nOur always-on world of social media may result in more connections, but they are superficial and can get in the way of building a real sense of belonging. Feeling bored may signal the desire for a greater sense of community and the feeling that you fit in with others around you. So take the step of joining an organization to build face-to-face relationships. You'll find depth that you won't get from your screen no matter how many likes you get on your post.\nSimilar to the need for belonging, bored people often report that they feel a limited sense of meaning. It's a fundamental human need to have a larger purpose and to feel like we're part of something bigger than ourselves. When people are bored, they're more likely to feel less meaning in their lives. If you want to reduce boredom and increase your sense of meaning, seek work where you can make a unique contribution, or find a cause you can support with your time and talent.\nIf your definition of boredom is being quiet, mindful, and reflective, keep it up. But if you're struggling with real boredom and the emptiness it provokes, consider whether you might seek new connections and more significant challenges. These are the things that will genuinely relieve boredom and make you more effective in the process.", "50": "Boredom has become trendy. Studies point to how boredom is good for creativity and innovation, as well as mental health. It is found that people are more creative following the completion of a tedious task. When people are bored, they have an increase in \"associative thought\"—the process of making new connections between ideas, which is linked to innovative thinking. These studies are impressive, but in reality, the benefits of boredom may be related to having time to clear your mind, be quiet, or daydream.\nIn our stimulation-rich world, it seems unrealistic that boredom could occur at all. Yet, there are valid reasons boredom may feel so painful. As it turns out, boredom might signal the fact that you have a need that isn't being met.\nOur always-on world of social media may result in more connections, but they are superficial and can get in the way of building a real sense of belonging. Feeling bored may signal the desire for a greater sense of community and the feeling that you fit in with others around you. So take the step of joining an organization to build face-to-face relationships. You'll find depth that you won't get from your screen no matter how many likes you get on your post.\nSimilar to the need for belonging, bored people often report that they feel a limited sense of meaning. It's a fundamental human need to have a larger purpose and to feel like we're part of something bigger than ourselves. When people are bored, they're more likely to feel less meaning in their lives. If you want to reduce boredom and increase your sense of meaning, seek work where you can make a unique contribution, or find a cause you can support with your time and talent.\nIf your definition of boredom is being quiet, mindful, and reflective, keep it up. But if you're struggling with real boredom and the emptiness it provokes, consider whether you might seek new connections and more significant challenges. These are the things that will genuinely relieve boredom and make you more effective in the process.", "51": "Can you remember what you ate yesterday? If asked, most people will be able to give a vague description of their main meals: breakfast, lunch, dinner. But can you be sure you've noted every snack bar in your car, or every handful of nuts at your desk? Most people will have a feeling that they've missed something out.\nWe originally had this suspicion back in 2016, puzzled by the fact that national statistics showed calorie consumption falling dramatically over past decades. We found reliable evidence that people were drastically under-reporting what they ate.\nNow the Office for National Statistics has confirmed that we are consuming 50% more calories than our national statistics claim.\nWhy is this happening? We can point to at least three potential causes. One is the rise in obesity levels itself. Under-reporting rates are much higher for obese people, because they simply consume more food, and thus have more to remember.\nAnother cause is that the proportion of people who are trying to lose weight has been increasing over time. People who want to lose weight are more likely to under-report their eating—regardless of whether they are overweight or not. This may be driven partly by self-deception or \"wishful thinking\".\nThe final potential cause is an increase in snacking and eating out over recent decades—both in terms of how often they happen and how much they contribute to our overall energy intake. Again, there is evidence that food consumed out of the home is one of the most poorly recorded categories in surveys.\nSo, what's the message conveyed? For statistics, we should invest in more accurate measurement options. For policy, we need to focus on options that make it easy for people to eat fewer calories. If people do not know how much they are eating, it can be really hard for them to stick to a diet. Also, we should be looking for new ways to ensure what people eat wouldn't have much impact on their waistlines. If this works, it won't matter if they can't remember what they ate yesterday.", "52": "Can you remember what you ate yesterday? If asked, most people will be able to give a vague description of their main meals: breakfast, lunch, dinner. But can you be sure you've noted every snack bar in your car, or every handful of nuts at your desk? Most people will have a feeling that they've missed something out.\nWe originally had this suspicion back in 2016, puzzled by the fact that national statistics showed calorie consumption falling dramatically over past decades. We found reliable evidence that people were drastically under-reporting what they ate.\nNow the Office for National Statistics has confirmed that we are consuming 50% more calories than our national statistics claim.\nWhy is this happening? We can point to at least three potential causes. One is the rise in obesity levels itself. Under-reporting rates are much higher for obese people, because they simply consume more food, and thus have more to remember.\nAnother cause is that the proportion of people who are trying to lose weight has been increasing over time. People who want to lose weight are more likely to under-report their eating—regardless of whether they are overweight or not. This may be driven partly by self-deception or \"wishful thinking\".\nThe final potential cause is an increase in snacking and eating out over recent decades—both in terms of how often they happen and how much they contribute to our overall energy intake. Again, there is evidence that food consumed out of the home is one of the most poorly recorded categories in surveys.\nSo, what's the message conveyed? For statistics, we should invest in more accurate measurement options. For policy, we need to focus on options that make it easy for people to eat fewer calories. If people do not know how much they are eating, it can be really hard for them to stick to a diet. Also, we should be looking for new ways to ensure what people eat wouldn't have much impact on their waistlines. If this works, it won't matter if they can't remember what they ate yesterday.", "53": "Can you remember what you ate yesterday? If asked, most people will be able to give a vague description of their main meals: breakfast, lunch, dinner. But can you be sure you've noted every snack bar in your car, or every handful of nuts at your desk? Most people will have a feeling that they've missed something out.\nWe originally had this suspicion back in 2016, puzzled by the fact that national statistics showed calorie consumption falling dramatically over past decades. We found reliable evidence that people were drastically under-reporting what they ate.\nNow the Office for National Statistics has confirmed that we are consuming 50% more calories than our national statistics claim.\nWhy is this happening? We can point to at least three potential causes. One is the rise in obesity levels itself. Under-reporting rates are much higher for obese people, because they simply consume more food, and thus have more to remember.\nAnother cause is that the proportion of people who are trying to lose weight has been increasing over time. People who want to lose weight are more likely to under-report their eating—regardless of whether they are overweight or not. This may be driven partly by self-deception or \"wishful thinking\".\nThe final potential cause is an increase in snacking and eating out over recent decades—both in terms of how often they happen and how much they contribute to our overall energy intake. Again, there is evidence that food consumed out of the home is one of the most poorly recorded categories in surveys.\nSo, what's the message conveyed? For statistics, we should invest in more accurate measurement options. For policy, we need to focus on options that make it easy for people to eat fewer calories. If people do not know how much they are eating, it can be really hard for them to stick to a diet. Also, we should be looking for new ways to ensure what people eat wouldn't have much impact on their waistlines. If this works, it won't matter if they can't remember what they ate yesterday.", "54": "Can you remember what you ate yesterday? If asked, most people will be able to give a vague description of their main meals: breakfast, lunch, dinner. But can you be sure you've noted every snack bar in your car, or every handful of nuts at your desk? Most people will have a feeling that they've missed something out.\nWe originally had this suspicion back in 2016, puzzled by the fact that national statistics showed calorie consumption falling dramatically over past decades. We found reliable evidence that people were drastically under-reporting what they ate.\nNow the Office for National Statistics has confirmed that we are consuming 50% more calories than our national statistics claim.\nWhy is this happening? We can point to at least three potential causes. One is the rise in obesity levels itself. Under-reporting rates are much higher for obese people, because they simply consume more food, and thus have more to remember.\nAnother cause is that the proportion of people who are trying to lose weight has been increasing over time. People who want to lose weight are more likely to under-report their eating—regardless of whether they are overweight or not. This may be driven partly by self-deception or \"wishful thinking\".\nThe final potential cause is an increase in snacking and eating out over recent decades—both in terms of how often they happen and how much they contribute to our overall energy intake. Again, there is evidence that food consumed out of the home is one of the most poorly recorded categories in surveys.\nSo, what's the message conveyed? For statistics, we should invest in more accurate measurement options. For policy, we need to focus on options that make it easy for people to eat fewer calories. If people do not know how much they are eating, it can be really hard for them to stick to a diet. Also, we should be looking for new ways to ensure what people eat wouldn't have much impact on their waistlines. If this works, it won't matter if they can't remember what they ate yesterday.", "55": "Can you remember what you ate yesterday? If asked, most people will be able to give a vague description of their main meals: breakfast, lunch, dinner. But can you be sure you've noted every snack bar in your car, or every handful of nuts at your desk? Most people will have a feeling that they've missed something out.\nWe originally had this suspicion back in 2016, puzzled by the fact that national statistics showed calorie consumption falling dramatically over past decades. We found reliable evidence that people were drastically under-reporting what they ate.\nNow the Office for National Statistics has confirmed that we are consuming 50% more calories than our national statistics claim.\nWhy is this happening? We can point to at least three potential causes. One is the rise in obesity levels itself. Under-reporting rates are much higher for obese people, because they simply consume more food, and thus have more to remember.\nAnother cause is that the proportion of people who are trying to lose weight has been increasing over time. People who want to lose weight are more likely to under-report their eating—regardless of whether they are overweight or not. This may be driven partly by self-deception or \"wishful thinking\".\nThe final potential cause is an increase in snacking and eating out over recent decades—both in terms of how often they happen and how much they contribute to our overall energy intake. Again, there is evidence that food consumed out of the home is one of the most poorly recorded categories in surveys.\nSo, what's the message conveyed? For statistics, we should invest in more accurate measurement options. For policy, we need to focus on options that make it easy for people to eat fewer calories. If people do not know how much they are eating, it can be really hard for them to stick to a diet. Also, we should be looking for new ways to ensure what people eat wouldn't have much impact on their waistlines. If this works, it won't matter if they can't remember what they ate yesterday."}, "questions": [{"id": "2021-06-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) announce", "B) commonplace", "C) confess", "D) desperate", "E) experience", "F) fascinated", "G) option", "H) prompted", "I) roughly", "J) routinely", "K) shining", "L) showering", "M) variety", "N) voyage", "O) wonder"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) experience\n名词\n【词性槽】引号内 store 后作复合名词\n【依据】为「购物体验」打分\nrate my \"store __(26)__\"，被要求打分的对象正是「store experience（购物体验）」——\n这是英语里固定说法。结合全文都在讲「给体验/服务打星」，experience 完全契合。\n【竞争词】M) variety 义不搭，不评「种类」\nvariety 是「种类、多样性」，「store variety」指商品种类，但作者通篇评的是服务/体验的星\n级，不是评商品多不多；与 rate 的对象不符。\nN) voyage 义不搭，航行无关\nvoyage 是「航行、海上旅程」，与在 EFTPOS 刷卡机上为购物打分的场景毫无关系，明显是干\n扰词。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) announce", "B) commonplace", "C) confess", "D) desperate", "E) experience", "F) fascinated", "G) option", "H) prompted", "I) roughly", "J) routinely", "K) shining", "L) showering", "M) variety", "N) voyage", "O) wonder"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) commonplace\n形容词\n【词性槽】the most 后、名词 activities 前作定语\n【依据】「最平常的」活动也被评分\nEven the most __(27)__ activities, such as calling Telstra or picking up a parcel…，连\n「最司空见惯的」活动——打个电话、取个包裹——都被要求评分。commonplace（平常\n的、司空见惯的）正好和 Even（连……都）形成「连最普通的也不放过」的强调。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) announce", "B) commonplace", "C) confess", "D) desperate", "E) experience", "F) fascinated", "G) option", "H) prompted", "I) roughly", "J) routinely", "K) shining", "L) showering", "M) variety", "N) voyage", "O) wonder"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) routinely\n副词\n【词性槽】be 与过去分词间修饰动词，要副词\n【依据】「例行地」跟进调查\nOnline purchases are __(28)__ followed up by a customer satisfaction survey，网购后\n「例行地、惯常地」会收到满意度调查——routinely（例行地）正好表达这种司空见惯、\n每次都来的常态，呼应全文「评分无处不在」。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) announce", "B) commonplace", "C) confess", "D) desperate", "E) experience", "F) fascinated", "G) option", "H) prompted", "I) roughly", "J) routinely", "K) shining", "L) showering", "M) variety", "N) voyage", "O) wonder"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) desperate\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词 so 后作表语，要形容词\n【依据】急切渴望星星\nCompanies are so __(29)__ for a hit of stars that if you delete the survey the\ncompany sends you another one.，公司「极度渴望」一点点好评，以至于你删了问卷它\n再发一份。desperate for（极度渴望……）是固定搭配，正好表达这种不顾一切的迫切。\n【竞争词】F) fascinated 搭配是 by，不搭 for\nfascinated（着迷的）一般搭 fascinated by/with，不说 fascinated for；而且「着迷」程度上\n达不到「删了再发」的那种迫切，与 so…that 的夸张语气不符。\nK) shining 义不搭，留给 32 题\nshining 是「闪亮的」，描述物体发光，不能描述公司「渴望」的心理状态；它更适合 32 题 a\n__(32)__ display of star feedback 修饰星星的「闪亮陈列」。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 F) —— fascinated 看着也像「热衷」。29 题 Companies are so\n__(29)__ for a hit of stars 处，fascinated（着迷的）词性对、也像「热衷好评」，颇具迷\n惑性。但它搭 by 不搭 for，且程度达不到「删了再发」的迫切。防坑：desperate for 才是\n固定搭配，fascinated 用 by。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) announce", "B) commonplace", "C) confess", "D) desperate", "E) experience", "F) fascinated", "G) option", "H) prompted", "I) roughly", "J) routinely", "K) shining", "L) showering", "M) variety", "N) voyage", "O) wonder"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) prompted\n动词\n【词性槽】be 后接过去分词作被动谓语\n【依据】被「催促/提示」去评分\nWe're __(30)__ to rate our apps when we've barely had a chance to use them，我们刚\n下载、还没怎么用，就被「催着/提示着」去给 app 打分。be prompted to do（被提示/\n被催促去做）是固定搭配，正好表达这种被系统弹窗催评分的场景。\n【竞争词】L) showering 搭配反向，且非分词形\nshower 表「倾泻、大量给予」常用 shower sb. with sth.，是「往某人身上倒东西」，方向\n是给予而非催促；这里需要的是「我们被催去评分」，be showering to rate 既不合搭配也讲\n不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) announce", "B) commonplace", "C) confess", "D) desperate", "E) experience", "F) fascinated", "G) option", "H) prompted", "I) roughly", "J) routinely", "K) shining", "L) showering", "M) variety", "N) voyage", "O) wonder"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) roughly\n副词\n【词性槽】数字前修饰百分比，要副词\n【依据】修饰数字表「大约」\ncompleted __(31)__ 2 per cent of it，作者强调「只完成大约 2%」就被要求评分，\nroughly（大约）恰好修饰具体数字 2 per cent，语义和搭配都顺。\n【竞争词】J) routinely 义不搭，不修饰数字\nroutinely 意为「例行地、惯常地」，修饰的是反复发生的动作，不能用来修饰一个具体数字 2\nper cent。说「例行地 2%」讲不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) announce", "B) commonplace", "C) confess", "D) desperate", "E) experience", "F) fascinated", "G) option", "H) prompted", "I) roughly", "J) routinely", "K) shining", "L) showering", "M) variety", "N) voyage", "O) wonder"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) shining\n形容词\n【词性槽】冠词 a 后、名词 display 前作定语\n【依据】「闪亮的」星级陈列\na __(32)__ display of star feedback has become the nuclear power source of the\nmodern economy，把星级好评比作现代经济的核电源，用 shining（闪亮的）修饰这种\n「星星陈列」既贴合星星发光的意象，又有夸张的修辞色彩。\n【竞争词】F) fascinated 只修饰人，不修饰物\nfascinated（着迷的）描述的是人「被吸引」的心理状态，不能修饰 display（陈列）这样的事\n物。「a fascinated display」不成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) announce", "B) commonplace", "C) confess", "D) desperate", "E) experience", "F) fascinated", "G) option", "H) prompted", "I) roughly", "J) routinely", "K) shining", "L) showering", "M) variety", "N) voyage", "O) wonder"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) wonder\n动词\n【词性槽】情态短语后接动词原形\n【依据】忍不住「纳闷/想知道」\nyou can't help but __(33)__ if these companies are basing their business on\nfabrications，作者忍不住「纳闷」这些公司是不是在拿捏造的数据做生意。wonder if（想\n知道是否、纳闷）是固定搭配，正好引出这个疑问。\n【竞争词】A) announce 义反，不接 if 表疑问\nannounce 是「宣布」，表示对外公开声明，与「忍不住产生疑问」的语气相反，也不和 if 从句\n构成「想知道是否」的搭配。\nC) confess 义偏，且要留给 34 题\nconfess 是「坦白、承认」，更适合 34 题 I __(34)__ that… 那种「我承认……」的坦白语境；\ncan't help but confess if… 语义也别扭。它要留给后面真正坦白的空。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 C) —— confess 也是动词易抢位。33 题 you can't help but\n__(33)__ if… 处，confess（坦白）词性也对、也像在「说什么」，容易被误填。但 wonder\nif（纳闷是否）才是固定搭配，且 confess 要留给 34 题。防坑：认准 wonder if 表内心疑\n问，confess 接的是 that 从句的招供。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) announce", "B) commonplace", "C) confess", "D) desperate", "E) experience", "F) fascinated", "G) option", "H) prompted", "I) roughly", "J) routinely", "K) shining", "L) showering", "M) variety", "N) voyage", "O) wonder"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) confess\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 I 后缺谓语，要动词\n【依据】坦白自己打分很随意\nI __(34)__ that with online surveys I just click the __(35)__ that's closest to my mouse\ncursor，作者要「坦白」一件不太光彩的事——自己评分时只是随手乱点。confess that\n（坦白、承认）正好引出这种自我招供的语气。\n【竞争词】A) announce 语气不符，非自我招供\nannounce（宣布）是正式对外公布，而这里是作者私下坦白自己的小毛病，带点不好意思的口\n吻。用「宣布我随便乱点」语气完全不对。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) announce", "B) commonplace", "C) confess", "D) desperate", "E) experience", "F) fascinated", "G) option", "H) prompted", "I) roughly", "J) routinely", "K) shining", "L) showering", "M) variety", "N) voyage", "O) wonder"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) option\n名词\n【词性槽】the 后作宾语，要名词\n【依据】点最近的「选项」\nI just click the __(35)__ that's closest to my mouse cursor(光标)，作者说自己随便点离\n光标最近的那个——评分时点的正是星级「选项」。option（选项）和 click、closest to\nmy mouse cursor 完全自洽。\n【竞争词】E) experience 已用且义不搭\nexperience（体验）已在 26 题用掉，且「click the experience」语义不通——你点的是屏幕\n上一个可点的东西，不是「体验」。\nM) variety 义不搭，无法被「点」\nvariety 意为「多样、种类」，「click the variety」讲不通——光标最近的不是「多样性」，而\n是一个具体可点的选项。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Being a close loser could greatly motivate one to persevere in their research.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「close loser」+「motivate to persevere」这组独特概念组合\n【定位】He thinks that being a close loser might give people a psychological boost, or the proverbial\nkick in the pants.\n他认为，作为险些落选的人，可能反而会给人一种心理上的推力，或者俗话说的那种「屁股\n上挨一脚」的督促。\n【改写】Being a close loser ↔ being a close loser\n术语逐字复现\ngreatly motivate one to persevere in their research ↔\nmight give people a psychological boost, or the proverbial kick in the pants\n抽象学院词 ↔ 形象俗语\n【辨邻】 J 本段是 Wang 用亲历的「论文先被拒后被更好期刊接受」轶事说明「losing did light\na motivating fire」，与「close loser ... greatly motivate」语义接近，但题干是对\n「close loser」这一群体作机制概括，本段只是个人例证。\nin his own experience, losing did light a motivating fire", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Grant awarders tend to favor researchers already recognized in their respective fields.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「Grant awarders」偏爱「already recognized」\n【定位】According to Barabasi, it's easier and less risky for those in positions of power to choose to\nhand awards and funding to those who've already been so recognized.\n根据 Barabasi 的看法，对掌权者而言，把奖项与资助交给那些已经被认可过的人，更省\n事、风险也更小。\n【改写】Grant awarders ↔ those in positions of power to choose to hand awards and funding\n上位「掌权者」收窄为下位「资助评审方」\ntend to favor ↔\nit's easier and less risky ... to choose to hand awards and funding to those who've already been\nso recognized\n「更容易、风险更小地选择给…」≈「倾向偏爱」\nresearchers already recognized in their respective fields ↔\nthose who've already been so recognized\n「already recognized」逐字复现\n【辨邻】 B 「the rich get richer」与「tend to favor」相关，但本段没解释「为何评审方倾向于\n把钱再给已被认可者」，而题干要的是这一机制本身——机制的展开在「Matthew\neffect」段。\nthe rich get richer", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Suffering early setbacks might help people improve their job performance.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】「early setbacks」改善「job performance」这一总结性命题\n【定位】But at the very least, the research is starting to show that early setbacks don't have to be fatal.\nThey might even make us better at our jobs.\n但至少，这些研究开始表明：早期的挫折未必致命，它们甚至可能让我们把工作做得更好。\n【改写】Suffering early setbacks ↔ early setbacks\n「early setbacks」逐字复现\nmight help people improve their job performance ↔ They might even make us better at our jobs\n情态词 might 一致 + 名词化与比较级同义\n【辨邻】 F 本段「the close losers performed even better than the narrow winners」是对\nWang 那项追踪研究的具体结论，没有把它扩展为「early setbacks 普遍可能\nimprove job performance」这种全文总结。题干用 people 这种泛指主语，匹配的\n应是收束全文的总结段。\nthe close losers performed even better than the narrow winners\nE 本段强调「Small early career setbacks seem to have a disproportionate effect\ndown the line」的负面后果与「made them weaker」，与「improve job\nperformance」的正向陈述极性相反。\nWhat didn't kill them made them weaker", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Research by social scientists on the effects of career setbacks has produced contradictory findings.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「social scientists」研究结论「contradictory」\n【定位】Studies in this area have found conflicting results.\n该领域的各项研究得出过相互矛盾的结论。\n【改写】Research by social scientists on the effects of career setbacks ↔\nOne way social scientists have probed the effects of career setbacks\n主语与对象逐字对应\nhas produced contradictory findings ↔ Studies in this area have found conflicting results\n「contradictory findings」≈「conflicting results」", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "It is not in the best interest of taxpayers to keep giving money to narrow winners.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「not in the best interest of taxpayers」+「narrow winners」\n【定位】After all, by continuing to pile riches on the narrow winners, the taxpayers are not getting the\nmaximum bang for their buck if the close losers are performing just as well or even better.\n毕竟，如果险些落选者表现得一样好甚至更好，那么继续把钱堆在险胜者身上，纳税人就没\n拿到他们花的钱该有的最大回报。\n【改写】not in the best interest of taxpayers ↔\nthe taxpayers are not getting the maximum bang for their buck\n习语翻译后与抽象短语语义等值\nto keep giving money to narrow winners ↔ by continuing to pile riches on the narrow winners\n「keep giving」≈「continuing to pile」\n【辨邻】 H 本段「the actual performance of the close losers was just as good」只是对\nWang 论文的同行评价，没替「纳税人」算账，与题干的核心视角不符。\nthe actual performance of the close losers was just as good", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Scientists who persisted in research without receiving a grant made greater achievements than those who got one with luck, as suggested in one study.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「persisted without receiving a grant」「greater achievements」「one study」\n【定位】But among those who stayed on, the close losers performed even better than the\nnarrow winners.\n但在那些坚持留下来的人里，险些落选者的表现甚至比那些险胜者更好。\n【改写】Scientists who persisted in research without receiving a grant ↔ among those who stayed on\n「persisted」≈「stayed on」\nmade greater achievements than those who got one with luck ↔\nthe close losers performed even better than the narrow winners\n「got one with luck」= narrow winners；「greater achievements」= performed\neven better\nas suggested in one study ↔\ntracked more than 1,100 scientists who were on the border between getting a grant and\nmissing out between 1990 and 2005\n「one study」指向 Wang 这项追踪研究\n【辨邻】 H 本段「the actual performance of the close losers was just as good」是 van de\nRijt 对 Wang 论文的复述与评价，并非这项研究本身的发现段。题干用「one study」\n+「suggested」更直接指向原研究段。\nthe actual performance of the close losers was just as good", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "A research paper rejected by one journal may get accepted by another.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「research paper rejected」「another」「accepted」这一具体轶事\n【定位】He submitted the unedited version to a more respected journal and got accepted.\n他把那份未经修改的稿子投到了一家更受尊敬的期刊，结果被接收了。\n【改写】A research paper rejected by one journal ↔\na recent paper he submitted to a journal, which accepted it only to request extensive editing,\nand then reversed course and rejected it\n「被拒」对应「最终又被退稿」\nmay get accepted by another ↔\nHe submitted the unedited version to a more respected journal and got accepted\n「被另一家接受」逐句对应", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "According to one recent study, narrow winners of research grants had better chances to be promoted to professors.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「narrow winners」「better chances to be promoted to professors」\n【定位】And the narrow winners were 50 percent more likely to be given a professorship.\n并且，那些险些胜出的人评上教授的概率比落选者高 50%。\n【改写】narrow winners of research grants ↔ those who just barely qualified for a grant\n「narrow winners」具体化为「刚够格者」\nbetter chances to be promoted to professors ↔\n50 percent more likely to be given a professorship\n「better chances」由「50% more likely」量化落地\nAccording to one recent study ↔\nA 2018 study published in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences\n「one recent study」对应那项 2018 年 PNAS 研究\n【辨邻】 D 本段「Others in the US have found similar effects with National Institutes of\nHealth early-career fellowships launching narrow winners far ahead of close\nlosers」只笼统说险胜者「far ahead」（远远领先），没量化「评教授概率高 50%」\n这种具体指标，对不上题干核心指纹。\nOthers in the US have found similar effects with National Institutes of Health early-career\nfellowships launching narrow winners far ahead of close losers", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "One researcher suggests it might be more fruitful to distribute grants on a relatively equal basis.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「distribute grants」「relatively equal basis」\n【定位】\"Maybe we should spend less money trying to figure out who is better than who,\" he said,\nsuggesting that some more equal dividing up of money might be more productive and\nmore efficient.\n「也许我们应当少花点钱去判断谁比谁更强，」他说，并建议把钱较为平均地分配，可能效\n率更高、更出成果。\n【改写】more fruitful ↔ more productive and more efficient\n「fruitful」≈「productive / efficient」\ndistribute grants on a relatively equal basis ↔ some more equal dividing up of money\n「relatively equal basis」≈「more equal」\nOne researcher suggests ↔\nsuggesting that some more equal dividing up of money might be more productive and more\nefficient\n「suggests」逐字复现\n【辨邻】 H 本段「He said the new finding is apparently reasonable and worth some\nattention」是 van de Rijt 对 Wang 论文的初步评价，没提出「distribute grants on\na relatively equal basis」这种政策主张——政策建议在下一段（I）才给。\nHe said the new finding is apparently reasonable and worth some attention", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Minor setbacks in their early career may have a strong negative effect on the career of close losers.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「Minor setbacks」「strong negative effect」「close losers」\n【定位】Small early career setbacks seem to have a disproportionate effect down the line.\n早期职业生涯里的小挫折，似乎会在后续产生不成比例的（巨大）影响。\n【改写】Minor setbacks in their early career ↔ Small early career setbacks\n「Minor」≈「Small」、「in their early career」≈「early career」\nmay have a strong negative effect ↔ seem to have a disproportionate effect down the line\n「disproportionate」+ 下文「weaker」共同等于「strong negative」\non the career of close losers ↔ This is bad news for the losers\n「close losers」对应本段开头「the losers」\n【辨邻】 B 本段「narrow winners become even bigger winners down the line」是对「赢家\n更赢」的中性描述，没有「disproportionate effect」这种「不成比例」的强度论断，\n与「strong negative effect」对不上。\nnarrow winners become even bigger winners down the line\nD 本段「launching narrow winners far ahead of close losers」虽提及「far ahead of\nclose losers」，但主语是「narrow winners」（赢家被推到远前方）；题干主语是\n「close losers」遭受负面影响，应取以输家为主语的段（E）。\nlaunching narrow winners far ahead of close losers", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What have studies found about boredom?", "options": ["A) It facilitates innovative thinking.", "B) It is a result of doing boring tasks.", "C) It helps people connect with others.", "D) It does harm to one's mental health."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What have studies found about boredom? 直接问研究的客观发现，未出现\ninfer/imply/mainly about 等推断或主旨信号\n【拆句】现在完成时一般疑问句 What have studies found about boredom?，主干是 studies\nhave found what about boredom；about boredom 是 found 的范围状语，结构平铺直\n叙，无陷阱\n【定位】Studies point to how boredom is good for creativity and innovation, as well as mental health.\n研究指出无聊有益于创造力和创新（思维），也有益于心理健康。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nboredom is good for creativity and innovation ↔ It facilitates innovative thinking.\n预判的「无聊带来好处+创新」两要素在 It facilitates innovative thinking. 中都被\n精确还原——facilitates 对应 is good for，innovative thinking 对应 creativity\nand innovation\nB) 与原文相反\n原文说 people are more creative following the completion of a tedious task——\n是完成无聊任务之后人更有创造力，并非无聊本身是做无聊任务的结果；It is a result of\ndoing boring tasks. 把因果方向倒过来，是典型的与原文相反\nC) 无中生有\nP1 谈的是无聊与创造力、创新、心理健康的关系，未提及无聊「帮人和他人连接」；It\nhelps people connect with others. 的内容在 P1 中没有任何对应句\nD) 与原文相反\n原文明确说无聊 as well as mental health（与心理健康并列在 good for 之后，意为对\n心理健康有益），It does harm to one's mental health. 说的是「有害」——评价极性\n完全反转", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the author say boredom might indicate?", "options": ["A) A need to be left alone.", "B) A desire to be fulfilled.", "C) A conflict to be resolved.", "D) A feeling to be validated."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say boredom might indicate? 是对作者陈述的直接发问，无\n推断或主旨信号\n【拆句】宾语从句 What does the author say boredom might indicate?，主句 the author say +\n宾语从句 boredom might indicate what；what 是宾语从句里 indicate 的宾语前移；结\n构常见，无陷阱\n【定位】As it turns out, boredom might signal the fact that you have a need that isn't being met.\n事实证明，无聊可能是在告诉你：你有一种需求没有被满足。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文未提「希望独处」；A need to be left alone. 是把后文 P3 关于「真实连接」的话\n题反向推断出来的伪命题，但 P2 中根本没有这层意思\nB) ✓ 正确\na need that isn't being met ↔ A desire to be fulfilled.\n预判的「需求+未被满足」两要素被 A desire to be fulfilled. 完整保留——desire 替换\nneed，to be fulfilled 替换被动定语从句\nC) 偷换概念\nA conflict to be resolved. 谈「冲突」，但 P2 全段未提任何 conflict（冲突）的概念\n——need 是「需求」不是「冲突」，两者不能等同\nD) 偷换概念\nA feeling to be validated. 说「需要被确认的感觉」，但原文说的是「需求未被满足」\n——「确认感觉」与「满足需求」不是一回事；validate 与 meet 不能等价替换", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What do we learn about social media from the passage?", "options": ["A) It may be an obstacle to expanding one's connections.", "B) It may get in the way of enhancing one's social status.", "C) It may prevent people from developing a genuine sense of community.", "D) It may make people feel that they ought to fit in with the outside world."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn about social media from the passage? 问文章告诉我们关于\nsocial media 的什么事实，无推断或主旨信号\n【拆句】一般疑问句 What do we learn about social media from the passage?，主干 we learn\nwhat about social media；from the passage 是状语，提示答案在文章里有明文，结构无\n陷阱\n【定位】Our always-on world of social media may result in more connections, but they are superficial\nand can get in the way of building a real sense of belonging.\n我们这个永远在线的社交媒体世界可能带来更多连接，但这些连接是肤浅的，会妨碍建立真\n正的归属感。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文 may result in more connections 说的是社交媒体带来更多连接（在数量上扩\n大），而 It may be an obstacle to expanding one's connections. 说它「妨碍扩大连\n接」——评价方向反转\nB) 偷换概念\n原文未提 social status（社会地位）；It may get in the way of enhancing one's\nsocial status. 中「妨碍提升社会地位」在 P3 中无任何对应——social 一词共用但\nstatus 完全没出现\nC) ✓ 正确\ncan get in the way of building a real sense of belonging ↔\nIt may prevent people from developing a genuine sense of community.\n预判的「妨碍」+「真正的归属感」两要素被 prevent people from developing a\ngenuine sense of community 完整对应——动词短语、形容词、核心名词全部同步同\n义替换\nD) 偷换概念\nIt may make people feel that they ought to fit in with the outside world. 说「让人\n觉得自己必须融入外部世界」，但原文 the feeling that you fit in with others around\nyou 把 fit in 作为「归属感」的一部分（是好事），不是说社交媒体让人觉得「必须\n迎合」", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does the author suggest people do to get rid of boredom?", "options": ["A) Count the likes they get on their posts.", "B) Reflect on how they relate to others.", "C) Engage in real-life interactions.", "D) Participate in online discussions."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author suggest people do to get rid of boredom? 问作者明文给\n出的建议，无推断信号\n【拆句】宾语从句 + 不定式状语 What does the author suggest people do to get rid of\nboredom?，主句 the author suggest + 宾语 people do what；to get rid of boredom\n是目的状语；结构稍复杂但无陷阱\n【定位】So take the step of joining an organization to build face-to-face relationships. You'll find\ndepth that you won't get from your screen no matter how many likes you get on your post.\n所以请采取行动，加入一个组织，去建立面对面的关系。你会从中获得屏幕给不了的深度\n——无论你的帖子收到多少点赞。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文 no matter how many likes you get on your post 明确否定「数点赞」的价值；\nCount the likes they get on their posts. 反而把「数点赞」当作建议——完全反向\nB) 答非所问\nReflect on how they relate to others. 说「反思自己如何与他人相处」，但作者的建议\n是「采取行动加入组织」，不是「反思」；reflect 与 take action 是两种完全不同的应\n对方式\nC) ✓ 正确\nbuild face-to-face relationships ↔ Engage in real-life interactions.\n预判的「面对面/线下」+「关系/互动」两要素被 Engage in real-life interactions. 整\n体保留，且未做任何延伸\nD) 与原文相反\nParticipate in online discussions. 说「参与线上讨论」——而原文恰恰让人远离屏\n幕，转向 build face-to-face relationships；线上与线下方向反转", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What should people do to enhance their sense of meaning?", "options": ["A) Try to do something original.", "B) Confront significant challenges.", "C) Define boredom in their unique way.", "D) Devote themselves to a worthy cause."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What should people do to enhance their sense of meaning? 直接问该做什么，无\n推断信号\n【拆句】情态动词疑问句 + 不定式状语 What should people do to enhance their sense of\nmeaning?，主干 people should do what；to enhance... 是目的状语；结构清晰，无\n陷阱\n【定位】If you want to reduce boredom and increase your sense of meaning, seek work where you can\nmake a unique contribution, or find a cause you can support with your time and talent.\n如果你想减少无聊、增强意义感，就去寻找一份你能做出独特贡献的工作，或者找到一项你\n能用时间和才华去支持的事业。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nTry to do something original. 说「做原创的事」，但原文 make a unique\ncontribution 强调的是「做独特贡献」——unique contribution（独特贡献）与\nsomething original（原创之物）不是同一概念，前者强调贡献的「不可替代性」，后\n者强调内容的「首创性」\nB) 张冠李戴\nConfront significant challenges. 说「直面重大挑战」，但 P4 通篇未提 challenge\n（挑战）；该词只在 more significant challenges 出现，是 P5 谈「摆脱无聊」的总建\n议，不是 P4 谈「增强意义感」的具体做法\nC) 答非所问\nDefine boredom in their unique way. 说「用自己独特的方式定义无聊」——这与题\n目所问的「增强意义感」无关；该项把 If your definition of boredom is being quiet,\nmindful, and reflective 中的 definition 一词嫁接到本题\nD) ✓ 正确\nfind a cause you can support with your time and talent ↔\nDevote themselves to a worthy cause.\n原文给的两个建议中，选项命中第二个 find a cause you can support with your time\nand talent——Devote themselves to a worthy cause. 是它的精准凝练", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What did the author suspect back in 2016?", "options": ["A) Calorie consumption had fallen drastically over the decades.", "B) Most people surveyed were reluctant to reveal what they ate.", "C) The national statistics did not reflect the actual calorie consumption.", "D) Most people did not include snacks when reporting their calorie intake."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What did the author suspect back in 2016 问的是一个具体过去时间点上的具体事实，没\n有 infer/imply 之类信号词，属于事实细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What did the author suspect back in 2016? 是一个简单的特殊疑问句，主语 the\nauthor，谓语 suspect，宾语用 what 提问，back in 2016 是时间状语，结构平铺直叙，无\n陷阱。\n【定位】We originally had this suspicion back in 2016, puzzled by the fact that national statistics\nshowed calorie consumption falling dramatically over past decades.\n我们最初是在 2016 年产生这种怀疑的——我们困惑于一个事实：国家统计数据显示，过去\n几十年里热量消费在大幅下降。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nCalorie consumption had fallen drastically over the decades. 复述的是统计数据本\n身的表象，作者恰恰是怀疑这个表象不可信，而非相信它。\nB) 无中生有\n原文只说人们 drastically under-reporting——漏报，没有任何文字表明他们 reluctant\nto reveal——主观上不愿透露；后文第 5 段更解释为自我欺骗或愿景式思维，而非拒绝\n回答。\nC) ✓ 正确\nnational statistics showed calorie consumption falling dramatically over past decades ↔\nThe national statistics did not reflect the actual calorie consumption.\n预判落点是“统计与现实不符”，四个选项里只有 The national statistics did not reflect\nthe actual calorie consumption. 直接说统计没有反映真实情况，与预判完全吻合。\nD) 张冠李戴\nMost people did not include snacks when reporting their calorie intake. 接近第 1\n段 every snack bar in your car 和第 6 段 snacking 的内容，但那些不是 2016 年的怀\n疑，而是后续展开的具体机制。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What has the Office for National Statistics verified?", "options": ["A) People's calorie intake was far from accurately reported.", "B) The missing out of main meals leads to the habit of snacking.", "C) The nation's obesity level has much to do with calorie intake.", "D) Calorie consumption is linked to the amount of snacks one eats."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What has the Office for National Statistics verified? 直问某机构核实了什么事实，无推\n断信号词，属事实细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What has the Office for National Statistics verified? 是现在完成时的特殊疑问句，\n主语是机构名，谓语 verified，结构清晰，无障碍。\n【定位】Now the Office for National Statistics has confirmed that we are consuming 50% more\ncalories than our national statistics claim.\n如今国家统计局已证实：我们实际摄入的热量比国家统计声称的高出 50%。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nwe are consuming 50% more calories than our national statistics claim ↔\nPeople's calorie intake was far from accurately reported.\nPeople's calorie intake was far from accurately reported. 把“实际比上报高出 50%”\n这件事精确概括为“摄入量远未被准确上报”，与预判一一对应。\nB) 无中生有\nThe missing out of main meals leads to the habit of snacking. 把“漏掉正餐”和“养成\n吃零食的习惯”绑成因果，原文从未提及这种因果链——文中说的是“忘记零食”而非“漏掉\n正餐导致吃零食”。\nC) 张冠李戴\nThe nation's obesity level has much to do with calorie intake. 涉及肥胖与热量摄入\n的关系，那是第 4 段 rise in obesity levels 后面的讨论，不是国家统计局证实的内容。\nD) 张冠李戴\nCalorie consumption is linked to the amount of snacks one eats. 类似第 6 段对零\n食的讨论，但那是作者推论的“潜在原因之一”，不是国家统计局已证实的事实。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What do we learn about obese people from the passage?", "options": ["A) They usually keep their eating habits a secret.", "B) They overlook the potential causes of obesity.", "C) They cannot help eating more than they should.", "D) They have difficulty recalling what they have eaten."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about obese people from the passage? 限定主体为某类人，问关于\n他们的文本陈述，仍是文中明示的细节而非推断。\n【拆句】题干 What do we learn about obese people from the passage? 是 What 引导的疑\n问句，about obese people 限定 learn 的对象，from the passage 是出处状语，句意\n直白。\n【定位】Under-reporting rates are much higher for obese people, because they simply consume more\nfood, and thus have more to remember.\n肥胖人群的漏报率要高得多，因为他们吃得本来就多，需要回想的食物也就更多。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nThey usually keep their eating habits a secret. 把客观上的漏报误读成主观上对外保\n密，原文没有任何表示故意隐瞒的字眼。\nB) 张冠李戴\nThey overlook the potential causes of obesity. 把“忽视肥胖原因”安到肥胖人群头\n上，但全文中“指出潜在原因”的是作者（We can point to at least three potential\ncauses），与肥胖人群无关。\nC) 过度推断\nThey cannot help eating more than they should. 加入了 cannot help（情不自禁）\n和 more than they should（超出应吃的量）两个主观限制，原文只是说 simply\nconsume more food——“就是吃得多些”，并未涉及自控力或应有量。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthey simply consume more food, and thus have more to remember ↔\nThey have difficulty recalling what they have eaten.\n预判落在“记不清吃了什么”，四个选项中只有 They have difficulty recalling what they\nhave eaten. 直接说回忆困难，与预判完美对应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What often goes unnoticed in surveys on food consumption?", "options": ["A) The growing trend of eating out.", "B) The potential causes of snacking.", "C) People's home energy consumption.", "D) People's changing diet over the years."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What often goes unnoticed in surveys on food consumption? 问的是调查中常被漏掉\n的某个具体类别，属于事实细节而非推断。\n【拆句】题干 What often goes unnoticed in surveys on food consumption? 主语 what，goes\nunnoticed 是系表结构（go + 形容词，表“处于…状态”），in surveys on food\nconsumption 是地点状语限定情境，结构无陷阱，关键要识别 go unnoticed 的固定搭配\n含义。\n【定位】Again, there is evidence that food consumed out of the home is one of the most poorly\nrecorded categories in surveys.\n再者，有证据表明：在家以外吃的东西，是调查中被记得最差的类别之一。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nfood consumed out of the home ↔ The growing trend of eating out.\nan increase in snacking and eating out over recent decades ↔\nThe growing trend of eating out.\n段末明确说在家外吃饭是漏报最严重的类别之一，只有 The growing trend of eating\nout. 把“外出就餐”作为答案给出，与定位句完全对应。\nB) 答非所问\n题干问“调查中常被漏掉的是什么”，The potential causes of snacking. 谈的是“吃零食\n的潜在原因”，方向跑偏；且原文也没说零食的“原因”被漏报。\nC) 偷换概念\nPeople's home energy consumption. 字面上像“家庭能源消费”，与“在家吃饭”完全是\n两个领域；原文谈的是 energy intake（能量摄入），不是 energy consumption（能\n源消费）。\nD) 以偏概全\nPeople's changing diet over the years. 用“饮食习惯随年份变化”这种大词把整篇主旨\n偷换上来，原文段落讲的是某一类食物在调查中被漏报，不是泛泛“饮食变化”。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author suggest policymakers do about obesity?", "options": ["A) Remind people to cut down on snacking.", "B) Make sure people eat non-fattening food.", "C) Ensure people don't miss their main meals.", "D) See that people don't stick to the same diet."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author suggest policymakers do about obesity? 问作者对决策者的具体\n建议，文中尾段已明示，属细节题而非主旨题。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author suggest policymakers do about obesity? 是 What 引导的\n疑问句，谓语 suggest 后接的是省略 that 的宾语从句 policymakers do ...，从句动词用原\n形 do（suggest 类动词的虚拟语气），about obesity 限定建议主题。\n【定位】Also, we should be looking for new ways to ensure what people eat wouldn't have much\nimpact on their waistlines.\n此外，我们应当寻找新方法，确保人们吃下去的东西不会对腰围产生多大影响。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nRemind people to cut down on snacking. 让人们少吃零食，仅来自 snacking and\neating out 的现象描述，文中并未在 P7 提出“提醒减少零食”这一具体政策建议。\nB) ✓ 正确\nwhat people eat wouldn\\'t have much impact on their waistlines ↔\nMake sure people eat non-fattening food.\nMake sure people eat non-fattening food. 把“吃了不会胖到腰围”换说成“吃不发胖的\n食物”，是从定位句的反向角度切入同一件事——与预判正对。\nC) 与原文相反\nEnsure people don\\'t miss their main meals. 让人们别漏掉正餐，与全文主旨方向相\n反——文章说人们漏报、吃得多，不是吃得少，更不存在“应该多吃几顿正餐”的建议。\nD) 偷换概念\nSee that people don\\'t stick to the same diet. 让人们不要长期保持同一种饮食，原\n文从未提及对单一饮食的看法；唯一相关的 stick to a diet 谈的是减肥者难以坚持节\n食，与让大众放弃单一饮食毫无关系。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2021-06-1"});
